act-label\">\n                        Contains\n                    </div>\n\n                    <h3>\n        <a href=\"/journey-to-the-west/fox-the-seventh\" class=\"entity-link\">\n            Fox the Seventh\n        </a>\n    </h3>\n\n                    <p>Part of</p>\n\n                    \n\n                    \n                </div>\n            \n                <div class=\"fact-card\">\n                    <div class=\"fact-label\">\n                        Contains\n                    </div>\n\n                    <h3>\n        <a href=\"/journey-to-the-west/garrison-commander-carp\" class=\"entity-link\">\n            Garrison Commander Carp\n        </a>\n    </h3>\n\n                    <p>Part of</p>\n\n                    \n\n                    \n                </div>\n            \n                <div class=\"fact-card\">\n                    <div class=\"fact-label\">\n                        Contains\n                    </div>\n\n                    <h3>\n        <a href=\"/journey-to-the-west/ge-xuan\" class=\"entity-link\">\n            Ge Xuan\n        </a>\n    </h3>\n\n                    <p>Part of</p>\n\n                    \n\n                    \n                </div>\n            \n                <div class=\"fact-card\">\n                    <div class=\"fact-label\">\n                        Contains\n                    </div>\n\n                    <h3>\n        <a href=\"/journey-to-the-west/general-fishbelly\" class=\"entity-link\">\n            General Fishbelly\n        </a>\n    </h3>\n\n                    <p>Part of</p>\n\n                    \n\n                    \n                </div>\n            \n                <div class=\"fact-card\">\n                    <div class=\"fact-label\">\n                        Contains\n                    </div>\n\n                    <h3>\n        <a href=\"/journey-to-the-west/golden-horned-king\" class=\"entity-link\">\n            Golden Horned King\n        </a>\n    </h3>\n\n                    <p>Part of</p>\n\n                    \n\n                    \n                </div>\n            \n        </div>\n    </details>\n</section>\n\n</section>\n\n\n<section class=\"did-you-know\">\n    <h2>Did you know?</h2>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Part of</li><li>Evidence around Journey to the West is classified as documented on Ton-o-Lore.</li><li>Journey to the West currently maps to 60 linked entities inside Journey to the West.</li>\n    </ul>\n</section>","overview":"<section class=\"overview\">\n    <h2>What is Journey to the West?</h2>\n    \n    <p>\n        Journey to the West (西遊記) is a Chinese novel published in the 16th century during the Ming dynasty and attributed to Wu Cheng&#039;en. It is one of the Four Great Classical Novels of Chinese literature.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n        The novel is an extended account of the legendary pilgrimage of the Tang dynasty Buddhist monk Xuanzang along the ancient Silk Road from China to India and back. Xuanzang traveled to the &quot;Western Regions&quot;, that is, Central Asia and India, to obtain Buddhist sacred texts (sūtras) and returned after many trials and much suffering. It retains the broad outline of Xuanzang&#039;s own account, Great Tang Records on the Western Regions, but the Ming dynasty novel adds elements from folk tales and the author&#039;s invention, for example, that Gautama Buddha gave this task to the monk and provided him with three protectors who agree to help him as an atonement for their sins. These disciples are Sun Wukong, Zhu Wuneng and Sha Wujing, together with a dragon prince who acts as Xuanzang&#039;s steed, a white horse.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n        Journey to the West has strong roots in Chinese folk religion, Chinese mythology, Daoist and Buddhist philosophy, and the pantheon of Daoist immortals and Buddhist bodhisattvas are still reflective of some Chinese religious attitudes today. Enduringly popular, the tale is at once a comic adventure story, an epic odyssey, a spring of spiritual insight, and an extended allegory in which the group of pilgrims journeys towards enlightenment by the power and virtue of cooperation.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n        The four protagonists, from left to right: Sun Wukong, Tang Sanzang (on the White Dragon Horse), Zhu Bajie, and Sha Wujing.\n    </p>\n    \n</section>","word_count":899,"uniqueness_score":"1.0000"},"entity":{"id":"5bec9f0b-d154-4ab0-9845-c60a3b4b57e9","name":"Journey to the West","slug":"journey-to-the-west","type":"topic","kind":"topic","type_label":"Topic","short_description":"Journey to the West (西遊記) is a Chinese novel published in the 16th century during the Ming dynasty and attributed to Wu Cheng'en. It is one of the Four Great Classical Novels of Chinese literature.","description":"Journey to the West (西遊記) is a Chinese novel published in the 16th century during the Ming dynasty and attributed to Wu Cheng'en. It is one of the Four Great Classical Novels of Chinese literature.\n\nThe novel is an extended account of the legendary pilgrimage of the Tang dynasty Buddhist monk Xuanzang along the ancient Silk Road from China to India and back. Xuanzang traveled to the \"Western Regions\", that is, Central Asia and India, to obtain Buddhist sacred texts (sūtras) and returned after many trials and much suffering. It retains the broad outline of Xuanzang's own account, Great Tang Records on the Western Regions, but the Ming dynasty novel adds elements from folk tales and the author's invention, for example, that Gautama Buddha gave this task to the monk and provided him with three protectors who agree to help him as an atonement for their sins. These disciples are Sun Wukong, Zhu Wuneng and Sha Wujing, together with a dragon prince who acts as Xuanzang's steed, a white horse.\n\nJourney to the West has strong roots in Chinese folk religion, Chinese mythology, Daoist and Buddhist philosophy, and the pantheon of Daoist immortals and Buddhist bodhisattvas are still reflective of some Chinese religious attitudes today. Enduringly popular, the tale is at once a comic adventure story, an epic odyssey, a spring of spiritual insight, and an extended allegory in which the group of pilgrims journeys towards enlightenment by the power and virtue of cooperation.\n\nThe four protagonists, from left to right: Sun Wukong, Tang Sanzang (on the White Dragon Horse), Zhu Bajie, and Sha Wujing.","evidence":"documented","universe":"journey-to-the-west","aliases":["Journey to the West"],"wikipedia_url":null,"image_url":null,"image_source":null,"image_credit":null,"image_license":null,"image_usage":null,"image_alt":null},"connections":[{"id":"993f603f-9f90-4b68-8938-a6ea82a9758d","relationship":"part_of","title":"Part of","explanation":"Part of","strength":"0.9000","discovery_score":"0.8500","direction":"incoming","from_entity_id":"d95ab09d-7220-4d52-84ad-080f73bc257e","to_entity_id":"5bec9f0b-d154-4ab0-9845-c60a3b4b57e9","entity_id":"d95ab09d-7220-4d52-84ad-080f73bc257e","slug":"wudang-mountain","name":"Wudang Mountain","type":"place","short_description":"Wudang Mountain guards the southeast of the Southern Continent and is the source of nine streams. On top of Wudang Mountain are thirty-six halls made from golden stone which thousands of pilgrims come to in order to…","evidence":"fiction","image_url":null,"path":"/journey-to-the-west/wudang-mountain","kind":"location","type_label":"Location"}],"rabbit_holes":[],"copyright":{"theme":"journey-to-the-west","title":"Journey to the West is not owned by Ton-o-Lore.","body":"Journey to the West, related names, marks, and materials are the property of Public-domain classic / respective modern adaptations and rights holders. Ton-o-Lore is an independent educational encyclopedia project and is not affiliated with or endorsed by those rights holders. Identification marks, when shown, are low-resolution images used under a fair-use rationale for commentary and identification only — not free or redistributable artwork."},"subject":{"id":"journey-to-the-west","name":"Journey to the West","theme":"journey-to-the-west","path":"/journey-to-the-west","logo":null,"categories":["folklore"]}}